---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Task Breakdown Skills - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="task-breakdowns" subChapterId="task-breakdown-skills">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        You've seen how providing context and referencing requirements improves
        task breakdowns. But if you're doing this regularly, you'll notice
        yourself repeating the same instructions: "follow the existing feature
        pattern," "size tasks for 2-3 days," "include acceptance criteria,"
        "reference the requirements doc." That repetition is a signal that it's
        time to build a skill.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you haven't read about skills yet, check out the <a
          href="/requirements-writing/skills"
          >Skills page</a
        > in the Requirements Writing chapter for the fundamentals. Here, we'll
        build on that foundation with a skill specifically designed for task breakdowns.
      </p>

      <h3>What a Task Breakdown Skill Does</h3>

      <p>
        A task breakdown skill packages all of your team's conventions for
        creating development tasks into a single set of instructions the AI
        loads on demand. Instead of typing out your preferences every time, you
        say "break down this feature into tasks" and the AI automatically knows:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Where to find requirements</strong> and
            how to read them
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">How to structure each task</strong> including
            which fields to include
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">How to size and scope tasks</strong> based
            on your team's capacity
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Where to save the output</strong> and what
            format to use
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Your team's preferences</strong> like vertical
            slicing, dependency tracking, and linking back to requirements
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Anatomy of a Task Breakdown Skill</h3>

      <p>
        Like any skill, this lives in a folder with a <code>SKILL.md</code> file.
        The description tells the AI when to activate it, and the instructions tell
        it how to do the work.
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap"
      >
        {
          `ticket-writing/
├── SKILL.md
└── references/
    └── templates.md`
        }
      </div>

      <p>The key sections of the SKILL.md include:</p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Trigger description
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            The frontmatter description tells the AI when to load this skill. Be
            specific about the triggers: "break down into tasks," "create
            tickets," "plan the work for this feature." Include the informal
            ways people phrase these requests too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Step 0: Understand the work
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Before generating any tasks, the skill instructs the AI to find and
            read the feature spec and any relevant decision records (so the tasks
            respect existing constraints), understand the codebase context (check
            AGENTS.md, look at existing patterns), and ask the user how they want
            to work through the breakdown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Breakdown philosophy
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            This is where your team's preferences go. Prefer vertical slices
            over horizontal layers? Tasks sized for 2-3 days? Feature ownership
            over isolated tasks? Explicit dependency tracking? Document these
            preferences once and the AI follows them every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Task template
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            A golden master for what each task should look like. Include the
            fields you care about: title, context, what to build, acceptance
            criteria, dependencies, and estimated scope. The AI will follow this
            structure consistently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Output location
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Where tasks get saved. In a <code>docs/tasks/</code> folder? As individual
            ticket files in <code>docs/tickets/</code>? As a single markdown
            file? Define the convention and the AI handles the file management.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Example Output</h3>

      <p>
        Here's what a task from the sample project's breakdown looks like. This
        was generated by the ticket-writing skill from the todo list
        requirements:
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap"
      >
        {
          `## Task 4: Build TodoCard Dashboard Widget

**Description**

Create a compact card component for the dashboard
home page that shows a quick summary of the user's
todos. It will display the count of active todos
and list the 5 most recently created incomplete items.

**Acceptance Criteria**

- [ ] TodoCard component at
      src/features/todos/TodoCard.tsx
- [ ] Displays a count of active todos
- [ ] Lists up to 5 most recent incomplete todos
- [ ] Each listed todo shows title and due date
- [ ] Includes a "View all" link to /todos
- [ ] Uses shadcn Card for consistent styling
- [ ] Updates reactively when todos change

**Files to Create or Modify**

- src/features/todos/TodoCard.tsx
- src/routes/index.tsx (add TodoCard to grid)

**Dependencies**

- Dashboard Shell Task 5 (home page with grid)
- Task 2 (useTodos hook)

**Estimated Scope:** Small (2-3 days)`
        }
      </div>

      <p>
        Notice how the task references specific file paths, names the components
        to use, lists concrete acceptance criteria, and maps out dependencies.
        This level of detail comes from the skill knowing about the project
        structure and the team's conventions.
      </p>

      <h3>Building Your Own</h3>

      <p>
        The fastest way to build a task breakdown skill is to start with one
        that's close to what you want and adapt it. The company maintains a
        shared skills repository with a ticket-writing skill you can use as a
        starting point:
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8"
      >
        <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2">
          Company Skills Repository
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Browse the collection of pre-built skills your team has put together.
          The ticket-writing skill is a good starting point for task breakdowns.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/konradgroup/ai-skills"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium"
          >
            github.com/konradgroup/ai-skills
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-3">
          <a
            href="/downloads/ticket-writing-skill.zip"
            download
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-violet-600 text-white text-sm font-medium rounded-md hover:bg-violet-700 transition-colors no-underline"
          >
            <span>&#8595;</span> Download the Sample Task Breakdown Skill
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>When adapting a skill, focus on these customizations:</p>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Adjust the task template</strong> to match
            your team's ticketing format. If you use Jira, the fields should map to
            your Jira issue types. If you track work in markdown files, design the
            format for readability.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Set your sizing conventions</strong>.
            Some teams size in hours, others in story points, others in t-shirt
            sizes. Whatever your team uses, define it in the skill so the AI
            estimates consistently.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800"
              >Define where requirements live</strong
            >. If your team always puts feature specs in <code
              >docs/features/</code
            >, tell the skill that. If they're in Confluence, explain how to
            find them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            4
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Include a golden master</strong>. Put
            an example of a well-written task breakdown in the <code
              >references/</code
            > folder. The AI will match the quality and detail of what you show it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Combining Skills for a Complete Workflow</h3>

      <p>
        The real power shows up when you chain skills together. A typical flow
        looks like this:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-orange-100 text-orange-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Requirements skill</strong> generates a
            structured requirements document from a conversation about a feature
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-orange-100 text-orange-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Task breakdown skill</strong> reads that
            requirements document and produces a set of development tasks
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-orange-100 text-orange-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Developer picks up a task</strong> and
            the AI already has full context from the AGENTS.md, requirements, and
            task description
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Each step in this chain feeds naturally into the next. The requirements
        skill produces output in a format the task breakdown skill knows how to
        read. The task breakdown skill produces output that gives developers
        clear starting points. No information is lost between handoffs because
        it's all in the repo.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="success" title="Mental model">
        <p>
          Think of skills as specialized team members. Your requirements skill
          is the business analyst who writes specs. Your task breakdown skill is
          the tech lead who turns specs into work items. Your AGENTS.md is the
          onboarding doc they all read on day one. They each do their job well
          because they have clear instructions, and they work together because
          the outputs are designed to chain.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          Don't try to build the perfect skill on the first attempt. Start
          simple: a basic task template and a few instructions about your
          conventions. Use it for a real breakdown, notice what's missing or
          wrong in the output, and update the skill. After 3-4 iterations,
          you'll have a skill that reliably produces task breakdowns your team
          can actually work from.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        In the <a
          href="/practical-exercise/get-started"
          >Practical</a
        > section, you'll use these skills hands-on with the sample project, taking
        a feature from requirements through task breakdown and into implementation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
